---
title: "What is Lesli? — Open-Source SaaS Framework for Ruby on Rails"
description: "Lesli is an open-source Ruby on Rails framework that provides reusable architecture, optional modules, and shared tools for building SaaS products and business software."
---

<header class="about-header">
    <%= partial("partials/lesli-navigation", locals: { logo: true }) %>

    <div class="container about-hero">
        <p class="about-eyebrow">What is Lesli?</p>
        <h1>A modular Rails foundation for <span class="lesli-title-colored">software built to grow.</span></h1>
        <p class="about-introduction">
            Lesli is an open-source framework built on Ruby on Rails for creating SaaS products, internal platforms,
            customer portals, and workflow software. It provides reusable architecture, shared conventions, and
            optional application capabilities—while your team keeps ownership of the code, data, integrations, and product.
        </p>

        <ul class="about-pillars" aria-label="Principles behind Lesli">
            <li class="about-pillar about-pillar--open">
                <span class="about-pillar-icon ri-open-source-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <strong>Open source by default</strong>
                <span>Use, inspect, adapt, and contribute to Lesli under the GPLv3 license.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="about-pillar about-pillar--rails">
                <span class="about-pillar-icon ri-code-box-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <strong>Rails-native</strong>
                <span>Work with standard Rails applications, engines, conventions, and tools.</span>
            </li>
            <li class="about-pillar about-pillar--ownership">
                <span class="about-pillar-icon ri-box-3-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                <strong>Built to be owned</strong>
                <span>Your code, data, workflows, integrations, and roadmap remain yours.</span>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</header>

<main class="about-content">
    <section class="about-purpose">
        <div class="container">
            <p class="about-section-label">The problem</p>
            <h2>Build the product—not the same foundation again.</h2>
            <div class="about-purpose-copy">
                <p>
                    Business software repeatedly needs accounts, users, permissions, navigation, auditing,
                    notifications, shared interface patterns, and other essential capabilities.
                </p>
                <p>
                    Lesli turns those repeatable concerns into a shared foundation and modular capabilities, so teams
                    can focus on the business model, workflows, and customer experience that make each product unique.
                </p>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-how" id="how-lesli-works">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="about-section-heading">
                <p class="about-section-label">How Lesli works</p>
                <h2>Start with Rails. Add only what you need.</h2>
                <p>
                    Your Rails application remains the host. Lesli supplies a shared foundation and reusable packages
                    that you can adopt as your product requires them.
                </p>
            </div>

            <div class="about-how-grid">
                <article>
                    <span class="ri-stack-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <h3>Lesli Core</h3>
                    <p>Shared architecture, conventions, services, and development workflows for Rails applications.</p>
                </article>
                <article>
                    <span class="ri-puzzle-2-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <h3>Engines</h3>
                    <p>Optional application capabilities such as access control, auditing, notifications, and support.</p>
                </article>
                <article>
                    <span class="ri-tools-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                    <h3>Gems and tools</h3>
                    <p>Reusable components, assets, utilities, testing support, and delivery automation.</p>
                </article>
            </div>

            <p class="about-how-note">
                Engines and gems are not mandatory layers. Choose the packages your application needs and keep
                product-specific workflows in your own code.
            </p>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-editorial">
        <div class="container">
            <div class="about-editorial-grid">
                <article>
                    <p class="about-section-label">What you can build</p>
                    <h2>Business software designed to evolve.</h2>
                    <p>
                        Lesli fits multi-account SaaS products, internal operations platforms, customer portals,
                        administration systems, workflow applications, and custom business systems—especially when
                        roles, domains, integrations, and history grow over time.
                    </p>
                </article>

                <article>
                    <p class="about-section-label">What it is—and isn't</p>
                    <h2>A framework, not a finished product.</h2>
                    <p>
                        Lesli is not a hosted service, no-code builder, admin template, or one-time boilerplate. It is
                        installed in a Rails application, where you choose official or custom modules and shape them
                        around the product you need to build.
                    </p>
                </article>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>

    <section class="about-closing">
        <div class="container">
            <p class="about-section-label">Start building</p>
            <h2>Start with structure. Build what makes your product different.</h2>
            <p>Use the shared foundation, choose only what you need, and keep ownership of the product you create.</p>
            <div class="about-actions">
                <a class="button-primary" href="/engines/lesli/start/">
                    Build with Lesli
                    <span class="ri-arrow-right-line" aria-hidden="true"></span>
                </a>
                <a class="button-primary-ghost" href="/engines/lesli/about/architecture/">
                    Explore the architecture
                </a>
                <a class="button-primary-ghost" href="https://demo.lesli.dev/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">
                    Try the demo
                </a>
            </div>
        </div>
    </section>
</main>
